{"id":74,"text":"On a rocky island far from the mainland, there lived an old man whose job was to keep a lighthouse. Every evening, he climbed two hundred steps to light the great lamp. Every morning, he climbed up again to clean it.\n\nA young sailor who had been rescued from a storm stayed with him for a few days. He watched the old man’s routine and finally asked, “Don’t you ever get bored? You do the same thing every day, and sometimes months pass without a single ship.”\n\nThe old man smiled. “Do you know how many ships have passed this island in thirty years?”\n\n“Thousands, I guess.”\n\n“Thousands,” said the keeper. “And I have never met most of the people on board. But every night, I know that someone out there is looking for my light. If I forget it, even once, a ship might hit the rocks.”\n\nThe sailor looked out at the dark sea. “I never thought about it like that. I only remember being thankful for a light when I was lost.”\n\n“That’s the secret of my job,” said the old man. “I don’t see the results, but I know they exist. Every night, I’m saving lives I will never know.”\n\nWhen the sailor left, he promised to come back. Years later, he returned as a captain, and he brought a new lamp as a gift.\n\nMoral: Meaningful work doesn’t always bring praise, but it still changes lives.","tr":null,"gl":{"on":"ustida, -da","a":"bir (noaniq artikl)","rocky":"toshloq","island":"orol","far":"uzoq","from":"-dan","the":"(aniq artikl)","mainland":"materik","there":"u yerda; bor","lived":"yashardi","an":"bir (noaniq artikl)","old":"keksa, eski","man":"odam, kishi","whose":"kimning","job":"ish, vazifa","was":"edi","to":"-ga; uchun","keep":"davom ettirmoq, saqlamoq","lighthouse":"mayoq","every":"har","evening":"kechqurun","he":"u (erkak)","climbed":"ko'tarildi, chiqdi","two":"ikki","hundred":"yuz","steps":"qadamlar; zinapoya","light":"yorug'lik; yengil","great":"buyuk, katta","lamp":"chiroq","morning":"ertalab, tong","up":"yuqoriga","again":"yana, qayta","clean":"toza; toz